{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PS2J1fxCNYFU4A9vuRGMme","type":"tool_result","content":"130\t**D8 \"Swift Justice\"**: 67 MB Swift; old groups backfilled to 10 ids; integer elf_class/machine `64`/`62`; eager symbol parsing\n131\tand xor brute force cost 60 s+; Mastery d01 STRIPPED → `[]`/`false`. **D9 \"Nimble\"** (86 s): two raw `HCFG` decoys, base64 blob,\n132\tNEW `hcfg_offset`. **D10 \"Zig Zag\"** (33 s): `.hcfg` section, NEW `has_hcfg_section`; Mastery Zig PE. **D11 \"D-Day\"** (36 s).\n133\t**D12 \"Pascal's Wager\"** (54 s, 2 wrong): HCFG RAW, raw-scan branch lacked its offset → bisect POSTs. **D13 \"Crystal Clear\"** (14 s).\n134\t**D14 \"Selective Memory\"** (140 Qs; 9 s, 0 wrong, carried-over solver). 17 KB GNUstep Objective-C ELF; HCFG RAW in a `.hcfg`\n135\tsection (file 0xfa0) → `has_hcfg_section` `true`; ObjC \"persistence descriptor\" theme unasked. TT 2.4 s / Mastery 3 sets 6.0 s.\n136\t\n137\t**D15 \"Stripped\"** (150 Qs; 74 s, 0 wrong): stripped + symbolized C ELF pair, identical `.text`/`.hcfg`; only novelty = the\n138\t`<qualifier>_` inside the field name (`_qualified(group, field)` router: file-name token -> member, `.symtab` fallback);\n139\t`machine` `kind: integer` -> `62`; \"reconstruct calling conventions\" unasked. TT 5.2 s; Mastery 5 sets 9.35 s.\n140\t\n141\t**D16 \"Wasm Up\"** (internal slug `webassembly-optimization-equivalence`; 160 Qs; GM CORRECT 91 s after start, 0 wrong POSTs;\n142\tTT + Mastery first try). Prompt: \"WebAssembly runs in a sandbox, but the sandbox has doors: imports, memory, indirect calls. Map\n143\tevery door in this module and inventory what passes through.\" New group = TWO wasm MVP modules from one source:\n144\t`d16--variant-000-compact.wasm` (561 B) and `-direct.wasm` (570 B). Sections type/function/memory(min 1)/export(`memory`,\n145\t`evaluate`)/code/data, LLVM-style 5-byte padded LEB128 sizes; ONE function `evaluate(i32)->i32` mapping 0/1/2 to constants --\n146\tcompact = `select` chain, direct = nested `if/else` (the \"optimization equivalence\"). NO import, table or element section.\n147\tData seg 0 (mem 1024) = RAW HCFG (`01 c1` = ver 1, key 0xc1), seg 1 (mem 2048) = trace-marker string.\n148\t- d16 ids: `sha256_1`/`_2` (archive order: direct, compact), `compact_`/`direct_` × format `wasm` + architecture `wasm32` (both\n149\t  `exact_text`), `compact_config_campaign/domain/mutex/url` only. No machine/hcfg_offset/import/export/table id.\n150\t- How it went: race-harness POSTs banked the 150 old ids at T+25 s and the shas + configs at T+27 s (generic qualifier router +\n151\t  raw HCFG scan already worked on wasm bytes); the worker added a 6-line `\\0asm` branch to the solver's container dispatch that\n152\t  calls the prebuilt `lib.binary.wasm_layout` (format `wasm`, machine `wasm32`), and the 4 label ids went CORRECT on the\n153\t  first try at T+91 s. The prebuild (`wasm-hcfg-data-segment`) paid for itself: no label guessing.\n154\t- The sandbox \"doors\" theme (imports, memory, indirect calls) was NOT asked -- 12th day the theme is decoration -- but note the\n155\t  module was built to have none of them; a later day may ask `imports`/`exports`/`memory_min`/`has_table` style ids.\n156\t- TT correct, 64 s in-window of which download 52 s (bundle 32 MB; the solve was 7.6 s); Mastery 5 sets / 800 ids, 23 s in-window\n157\t  (download 14.6 s, solve 2.9 s); Mastery set1 d01 STRIPPED again, set1 d02 a PE.\n158\t\n159\t## GM answer conventions learned\n160\t| Field family | Accepted form | Rejected (never resubmit) |\n161\t|---|---|---|\n162\t| elf_class (`kind` exact_text) | `ELF64` / `ELF32` (readelf style) | ELFCLASS64 |\n163\t| elf_class (`kind: integer`, D8) | the bit width `64` / `32` | `2` / `1` (raw EI_CLASS byte) |\n164\t| machine (`kind: integer`, D8-D9, D15) | decimal e_machine (`62` = x86-64; u16 at offset 18 in EI_DATA order) | — |\n165\t| endianness | `little` / `big` | little endian, lsb, msb |\n166\t| machine (ELF) | `x86-64`, `x86`, `AArch64`, `ARM` | EM_X86_64, x86_64, amd64 |\n167\t| machine (PE FILE_HEADER) | 0x14c → `i386`; 0x8664 → `x86-64` | x86 (for PE i386); raw `0x8664` |\n168\t| architecture | the same machine words (Go ELF amd64 → `x86-64`; Go PE 0x8664 → `x86-64`) | amd64, arm64, 386, x86_64 |\n169\t| elf_type | the constant `ET_EXEC` / `ET_DYN` | EXEC |\n170\t| entry_point, crc32_hex, xor_key_hex | lowercase hex with `0x` (crc `0x%08x`, key `0x%02x`) | — |\n171\t| recover_code | base-10 integer (`mov eax, 0x1fa4` → 8100); `none` if the named function is absent and the format allows it | the renamed function's constant |\n172\t| has_symtab / has_cpp_* | enum `true`/`false` | — |\n173\t| marker | exact `.huntress` string without the NUL; `none` if the section is missing | — |\n174\t| format (native) | lowercase container: `elf`, `pe` (`macho` presumed) | ELF, ELF64, elf64-x86-64, HCFG, json, exe, pe32+, coff, mz |\n175\t| format (.NET) | `pe_cli` | ~50 synonyms — lib/knowledge.py DOTNET_FORMAT_REJECTED |\n176\t| format (Java/Kotlin jar) | `jar` (D6 Java, D7 Kotlin) | jar_jvm |\n177\t| format / architecture (Swift, Nim, Zig, D, FPC, Crystal, ObjC ELF, stripped/symbolized C pair; D8-D15) | `elf` / `x86-64` (source language never changes the container label) | — |\n178\t| format / architecture (WebAssembly module, D16, exact_text) | `wasm` / `wasm32` (lowercase; first try) | — (not tried: WebAssembly, wasm-mvp, wasm32-unknown) |\n179\t| C++ symbols (ELF) | bare Itanium mangled name (`_ZNK...`, `_ZTV...`) | the demangled form |\n180\t| C++ symbols (PE/MinGW) | COMDAT section name `.text$<mangled>` (function), `.rdata$<mangled>` (vtable) | bare mangled name |\n181\t| section_names (JSON) | pyelftools order INCLUDING the leading `\"\"` | list without \"\" |\n182\t| symbols (JSON) | non-empty .symtab names, table order, dupes kept | list with the \"\" entries |\n183\t| disassembly (JSON) | capstone intel `\"mov eax, 8100\"`, `\"ret\"`, decimal immediates | hex imm, `\"ret \"`, `mov eax,0x..` |\n184\t| metadata_version | BSJB root string `v4.0.30319` | net8.0 / TFM / assembly version |\n185\t| config_* | values verbatim from the decoded HCFG JSON (mutex keeps ONE backslash) | JSON-escaped `\\\\` |\n186\t| hcfg_version | header byte 4 as a decimal integer | — |\n187\t| has_hcfg_section (D10, D14, enum) | `true` when a section is literally named `.hcfg` (Zig ELF/PE, ObjC ELF) | — |\n188\t| hcfg_offset (`kind: integer`, D9, D11-D13) | decimal FILE offset of the stored blob's first byte: base64 text start (`30696`, `368272`, `560188`), RAW = the `HCFG` magic (`82928`) | — (not tried: VA, struct start, `strings -t d` offset-1) |\n189\t| xor_key_hex | HCFG header byte 5 as `0x%02x` | — |\n190\t| compression_methods (jar, `kind: json`) | sorted distinct ZIP `compress_type` ids, compact: `[0,8]` | — |\n191\tNaming law (from playbook.py): native format = container only; a managed .NET binary = container_runtime from the spec tokens (pe+cli).\n192\tJava broke the compound law (`jar`), so when a label is new, try the plain container name AND the compound, one per POST.\n193\t\n194\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n195\t- **A multi-field POST is all-or-nothing.** One wrong field fails the set and hides which field was wrong (D2: correct `pe` looked\n196\t  rejected for hours). Submit unconfirmed/new fields ALONE; batch only the known ones.\n197\t- **Guessing synonyms for a synthetic label** (D3 .NET format: ~50 tries, ~1.5 h). Derive from spec tokens and the playbook naming laws,\n198\t  use `hctf labels`, and read the question `syntax` first: the GM embeds hints.\n199\t- **Chasing the prompt's theme** (D5-D15): the language flavour (selectors, protocols, coroutines, record layouts, D15's \"reconstruct\n200\t  stripped functions from calling conventions\") has never been asked since D4. Read the contract ids first; reverse only what an id asks for.\n201\t- **Shell/JSON quoting** of `Global\\Huntress...` mutexes: build the submit JSON with python/jq, not by hand in a shell string.\n202\t- **capstone `ret` has an empty op_str**: `f\"{m} {o}\"` gives `\"ret \"`. Strip it, and convert immediates to decimal.\n203\t- **Cumulative zip**: old groups' answers carry over when their sha256 match; spend the time on the NEW group only. POST the\n204\t  known ids first (they bank as `accepted`), then the new group alone (D15: 140 banked at T+17 s, the 8 new ids at T+74 s).\n205\t- **A new id shape on an old field** (D15 `analysis_stripped_machine`): a field-table lookup on the whole suffix misses it. Peel\n206\t  a member qualifier off the front before declaring the field unknown; never answer it from the \"first\" member of the group.\n207\t- **Recursive unzip** destroys nested containers (.jar/.apk) and their `dNN--` prefix; keep them whole.\n208\t- **Same field name, different `kind`** (D8 elf_class/machine): read `answer_format.kind` per id.\n209\t- **Huge members** (D8: 67 MB Swift): any per-key xor brute force or per-field re-parse blows a timed window. Cheap encodings first\n210\t  (raw magic, base64 `SENGR`), crc-gate every hit, parse each member once.\n211\t- **Decoy magics** (D9 Nim): code that CHECKS the magic embeds `\"HCFG\"` literals. Empty len 0 => crc32(b\"\")==0 passes! Require\n212\t  non-empty JSON that parses, keep scanning, then try the base64 form.\n213\t- **Location fields per scan path** (D12): a decoder with raw AND base64 branches must return the hit offset from BOTH. Before\n214\t  submitting, assert no `none` where a config was decoded.\n215\t- **`strings -t d` offsets on Nim/Swift literals**: a printable byte before the text shifts the offset by 1. Use `grep -abo` /\n216\t  `re.finditer(...).start()` on the exact token.\n217\t- **Jar members are deflated**: `grep HCFG file.jar` finds nothing. Inflate the class, read constant-pool strings, base64-decode.\n218\t- **Stripped builds / renamed functions** in variants (D1 TT, D2 Mastery set2, D8 Mastery d01): answer what the question defines\n219\t  for absence (`[]`, `none`, has_symtab=false); never substitute a heuristic constant.\n220\t- **Speed flags measured under load** (D12, D14): \"N s on single_solve\" from a parallel solver-test is contention; re-measure\n221\t  pinned and isolated before touching code.\n222\t- **Tool parity**: ilspycmd, dotnet, rustfilt, GoReSym, rabin2, swift-demangle are in `bin/`; monodis/de4dot are not.\n223\t- **Timed windows** (6-30 s; download is a big share of a 3-5 set Mastery): early TT/Mastery expiries were download/orchestration\n224\t  latency, not solver speed. After editing hctf/*.py, restart the orchestrator (D1/D2 ran stale modules).\n225\t- **WASM parsing**: section/segment sizes are 5-byte PADDED LEB128 (`86 80 80 80 00`); a parser that assumes 1-byte sizes or\n226\t  minimal LEBs misreads every offset. Read LEB128 generically (`lib.binary.wasm_layout`); validate the whole module, not just the magic.\n227\t- **PE machine map must include 0x8664 → `x86-64`**: a from-scratch replay once emitted `0x8664` and failed TT twice.\n228\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f4eac315-82ea-4fe2-9167-1391c6fe3697","uuid":"454124a9-9433-4115-9445-5566870eb174","timestamp":"2026-10-16T13:19:31.522Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md","content":"**D8 \"Swift Justice\"**: 67 MB Swift; old groups backfilled to 10 ids; integer elf_class/machine `64`/`62`; eager symbol parsing\nand xor brute force cost 60 s+; Mastery d01 STRIPPED → `[]`/`false`. **D9 \"Nimble\"** (86 s): two raw `HCFG` decoys, base64 blob,\nNEW `hcfg_offset`. **D10 \"Zig Zag\"** (33 s): `.hcfg` section, NEW `has_hcfg_section`; Mastery Zig PE. **D11 \"D-Day\"** (36 s).\n**D12 \"Pascal's Wager\"** (54 s, 2 wrong): HCFG RAW, raw-scan branch lacked its offset → bisect POSTs. **D13 \"Crystal Clear\"** (14 s).\n**D14 \"Selective Memory\"** (140 Qs; 9 s, 0 wrong, carried-over solver). 17 KB GNUstep Objective-C ELF; HCFG